<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caracalla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">188–<a href="/years/217">217</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/severan-dynasty">Severan dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Caracalla was Roman emperor from 198, jointly with his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> and then his brother Geta, and sole ruler from 211 to 217 after having Geta murdered. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutio-antoniniana">Constitutio Antoniniana</a> of 212 granted Roman citizenship to almost all free inhabitants of the empire, and he built the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baths-of-caracalla">Baths of Caracalla</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. He was assassinated near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carrhae">Carrhae</a> while campaigning against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caracalla.jpg" alt="Caracalla" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Pol GRANDMONT · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0_Caracalla_-_Museo_Massimo_alle_Terme.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Caracalla was born Lucius Septimius Bassianus at Lugdunum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, the elder son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julia-domna">Julia Domna</a>. At seven he was renamed Marcus Aurelius Antoninus as part of his father's attempt at union with the families of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius">Antoninus Pius</a> and Marcus Aurelius.</p><p>His father, acclaimed Augustus in 193, had himself adopted posthumously by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a>, and Caracalla was made Caesar in 195 or 196. On 28 January 198, aged nine, he became joint Augustus, on the day of his father's triumph over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a>, and in 202 he was consul with his father.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Severus died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eboracum">Eboracum</a> on 4 February 211 while on campaign in Caledonia, leaving Caracalla and his brother Geta as joint rulers. On 26 December 211, at a reconciliation meeting arranged by their mother, Geta was killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/praetorian-guard">Praetorian Guards</a> loyal to Caracalla, and it is widely accepted that Caracalla ordered it. He persecuted Geta's supporters, and the Senate pronounced a damnatio memoriae on Geta, whose name it became a capital offence to speak or write; an estimated 20,000 people were massacred. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julia-domna">Julia Domna</a> took on more of the government, since Caracalla found administration mundane.</p><p>In 212 he issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutio-antoniniana">Constitutio Antoniniana</a>, granting full Roman citizenship to all free inhabitants of the empire except the dediticii. In 213 he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, never to return, and campaigned against the Alamanni on the German frontier, defeating some tribes and settling others by diplomacy. In December 215 he slaughtered a deputation of leading citizens at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> after the city mocked his claim to have killed Geta in self-defence, and set his troops loose on the city for several days of looting.</p><p>He raised the annual pay of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> from 2,000 sesterces to 2,700 to 3,000, and the cost led him to debase the coinage. In 215 he introduced the antoninianus, intended as a double <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denarius">denarius</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 216 Caracalla began a campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> after King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artabanus-iv">Artabanus IV</a> refused a marriage proposal. On 8 April 217, travelling from Edessa to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carrhae">Carrhae</a> to visit a temple of the moon god Sin, he was stabbed by a Roman soldier, Justin Martialis, and the two Praetorian tribunes completed the killing. Macrinus succeeded him three days later, and had him deified.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a>, Herodian and the Historia Augusta portray him as a cruel tyrant and a soldier first and an emperor second, a view that has largely persisted. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutio-antoniniana">Constitutio Antoniniana</a> goes almost unnoticed in the classical records. In the late 18th century French painters revived his image, as the tyranny ascribed to him seemed to parallel that ascribed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Caracalla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to Cassius Dio, the emperor <b>Caracalla</b> campaigned against them in 213, although some scholars argue that the name was added by a later excerptor and that the first certain mention comes in a Latin panegyric of 289.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Caracalla</b> boasted around 214/215 of his enmity with the Marcomanni, Jordanes believed they paid tribute to the Gothic king Ostrogotha, and Diocletian claimed a triumph over them in 299.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marcomanni">Marcomanni · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>To limit the power of a single governor with three legions, the province was divided into Britannia Superior, ruled from London, and Britannia Inferior, ruled from York, a reform traditionally credited to Septimius Severus but perhaps completed under <b>Caracalla</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Most fighting was internal: riots between Greeks and Jews in Alexandria from 38 CE, the Jewish revolt of 115–117 that destroyed the Alexandrian Jewish community, the revolt of the herdsmen known as the Boukoloi in the Delta in the early 170s, and <b>Caracalla</b>'s massacre of Alexandrians in 215.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Septimius Severus and <b>Caracalla</b> restored it in 202, 'damaged by age, with all its adornments', and added their own inscription.</blockquote><a href="/work/pantheon">Pantheon · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Until 212 CE most provincials were free non-citizens (peregrini) living under their own local law; that year the Constitutio Antoniniana of the emperor <b>Caracalla</b> made almost all free men citizens.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-rome">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Rome</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lugdunum, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Carrhae, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.76,4.82&amp;year=206" aria-label="Lugdunum on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4664 551 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Caracalla"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5114 821h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5114 821h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.76,4.82&amp;year=206">What was here in 206 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
